Bash Notes
IFS Variables
----------------

IFS is a special variable built into bash that bash uses to determine how to break up arguments, split words, and do all sorts of stuff.

If you mess with IFS it can cause issues.

It is still useful to be aware of.

If the IFS is unset, it defaults to acting like normal default IFS.

IFS stands for Internal Field Separator.

The first char of the IFS is what then becomes the separator of a stringified, quoted array.
(And in other instances too)

Use parenthesis () to create a sub-shell or local scope variables within the parenthesis.

You can use "${!array[*]}" to get the keys (indices) even in indexed arrays.
